---
import TechniquePost from "../../layouts/TechniquePost.astro"
import TabsBuild from "../../components/UnderTheHood/TabsBuild.astro"
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components"
---

<TechniquePost slug="tabs-radio-buttons">
  <section>
    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Tabs usually mean JavaScript: click handlers, <code>aria-selected</code>,
      hiding and showing panels. CSS-only tabs with radios are old news, but
      they needed the labels grouped apart from the panels and one selector per
      tab to connect them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="the-trick">The trick</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        One radio group holds the state, and arrow keys move between the radios
        natively
      </li>
      <li>
        The markup interleaves radio, label, panel for each tab, so <code
          >:checked + label + panel</code
        > shows the right panel without a selector per tab
      </li>
      <li>
        A wrapping flex row with <code>order</code> pulls every label into one row
        and drops the panels below
      </li>
      <li>
        The radios are visually hidden, <code>:focus-visible + label</code> draws
        the focus ring on the label's pill
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>:nth-child(1 of .tab-label)</code> finds the first label among the radios
        and panels, to round the outer corners of the track
      </li>
    </ul>
    <Code
      lang="css"
      code={`.tabs {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
}

.tab-label {
  order: 1;
}

.tab-panel {
  display: none;
  inline-size: 100%;
  order: 2;
}

.tab-input:checked + .tab-label + .tab-panel {
  display: block;
}

.tab-label:nth-child(1 of .tab-label) {
  border-end-start-radius: var(--radius);
  border-start-start-radius: var(--radius);
}`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="try-it">Try it</h2>
    <TabsBuild />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="fallback">Fallback</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Browsers without <code>:nth-child(of S)</code> drop those rules: the first
        and last tabs lose their outer rounding, switching still works
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>order</code> only moves the tabs visually. Focus follows the DOM, which
        already has the right order: the checked radio, then the content of its open
        panel. No <code>reading-flow</code> needed
      </li>
      <li>
        The rest is <code>:checked</code>, the next-sibling combinator and <code
          >order</code
        >, supported everywhere
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</TechniquePost>
